== 2. False Definitions of Grace

#strong[The second #emph[device] that Satan has to keep souls in a sad, doubting, and questioning condition, is,]

BY leading them to make false definitions of their graces. Satan knows that as false definitions of sin wrong the soul one way, so false definitions of grace wrong the soul another way.

I will give an instance only in faith. Oh, how Satan labors with all his might to lead men to make false definitions of faith! Some he leads to define faith too high: that it is a full assurance of the love of God to a man’s soul in particular, or a full persuasion of the pardon and remission of a man’s own sins in particular. Satan says, “Why do you talk of faith? Faith is an assurance of the love of God, and of the pardon of sin, and this you do not have. You know you are far from this; therefore you have no faith.” And by drawing men to make such a false definition of faith, he keeps them in a sad, doubting, and questioning condition, and makes them spend their days in sorrow and sighing, so that tears are their drink, and sorrow is their food, and sighing is their work all the day long, etc.

The philosophers say there are eight degrees of heat; we discern three. Now if a man were to define heat only by the highest degree, then all the other degrees would be cast out from being heat. So if men define faith only by the highest degrees, by assurance of the love of God, and of the pardon of his sins in particular, what will become of the lesser degrees of faith?

If a man were to define a living man only by the highest and strongest signs of life, such as laughing, leaping, running, working, walking, etc., would not many thousands who groan under inward and outward weaknesses, and who cannot laugh, nor leap, nor run, nor work, nor walk, be found dead men by such a definition, though we know them to be alive? It is so here, and you know how to apply it, etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that there may be true faith, yes, great measures of faith, where there is no assurance. The Canaanite woman in the Gospel had strong faith, yet no assurance that we read of. “I have written these things to you#super[+]” (says John) “who believe in the name of the Son of God, so that you#super[+] may know that you#super[+] have eternal life, and so that you#super[+] may believe in the name of the Son of God.” In these words you see that they did believe, and had eternal life: in respect of the purpose and promise of God; and in respect of its seeds and beginnings in their souls; and in respect of Christ their head, who sits in heaven as a public person, representing all his chosen ones: “God raised us up with Christ#footnote[Eph 2:6.] and seated us with Him in the heavenly realms in Christ Jesus.” And yet they did not know that they had eternal life. It is one thing to have a right to heaven, and another thing to know it; it is one thing to be beloved, and another thing for a man to know that he is beloved. It is one thing for God to write a man’s name in the book of life, and another thing for God to tell a man that his name is written in the book of life, and to say to him, “Rejoice that your name is written in heaven.”

So Paul: “In Him you#super[+] also trusted, after#footnote[Eph 1:13. So those in Isa 50:10 had faith, though they had no assurance. Mic 7:8, 9.] you#super[+] heard the word of truth, the gospel of your#super[+] salvation; in Him also, after you#super[+] believed, you#super[+] were sealed with the promised Holy Spirit.”

So Micah: “Do not gloat over me, my enemy! Though I have fallen, I will arise; though I sit in darkness, the #smallcaps[Lord] will be my light. Because I have sinned against Him, I must endure the indignation of the #smallcaps[Lord],” etc.; or “the sad countenance of God,” as the Hebrew has it. This soul had no assurance, for he sits in darkness, and was under the sad countenance of God; and yet he had strong faith, as appears in these words: “Though I have fallen, I will arise; though I sit in darkness, the #smallcaps[Lord] will be my light. … He will bring me out into the light, and I will see His righteousness.” Let this suffice for the first answer.

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that God in the Scriptures defines#footnote[John 1:12.] faith otherwise. God defines faith as a receiving of Christ: “To all who did receive Him, He gave the right to become#footnote[Acts 11:23.] children of God, to those who believed in His name.” He defines it as a cleaving of the soul to God, though no joy, but afflictions, attend the soul. Yes, the Lord defines faith as a coming to God in Christ,#footnote[Matt 11:28. John 6:37. Heb 7:25, 26. Isa 26:3, 4, etc.] and often as a resting, and staying, or rolling of the soul on Christ. It is safest and sweetest to define both vices and graces as God defines them. This is the only way to settle the soul, and to secure it against all the wiles of men and devils, who labor by false definitions of grace to keep precious souls in a doubting, staggering, and languishing condition, and so make their lives a burden, a hell to them.

The third #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider seriously that there may be true faith where there#footnote[Matt 6:30 ch. 14:31 and ch. 16:8.] are many doubts. Witness those frequent sayings of Christ to his disciples: “You#super[+] of little faith, why are you#super[+] so afraid?”#footnote[Luke 12:28.] Persons may be truly believing who nevertheless are at times doubting. In the same persons (whom the Scriptures mentioned above speak of) you may see their faith commended and their doubts condemned, which necessarily presupposes that both are present.

The fourth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that assurance is an effect of faith; therefore it cannot be faith. The cause cannot be the effect, nor the root the fruit. As the effect flows from the cause, the fruit from the root, the stream from the fountain, so assurance flows from faith. I will prove this truth as follows.

The assurance of our salvation and of the pardon of sin arises primarily from the witness of the Spirit of God that#footnote[Eph 1:13. Gal 5:6.] we are the children of God. And the Spirit never witnesses this until we are believers, “for we are sons by faith in#footnote[Gal 4:6.] Christ Jesus.” Therefore assurance is not faith, but follows it, as the effect follows the cause.

Again, no man can be assured and persuaded of his salvation until he is united to Christ, until he is grafted into Christ; and a man cannot be grafted into Christ until he has faith. He must first be grafted into Christ by faith before he can have assurance of his salvation, which clearly shows that assurance is not faith, but an effect and fruit of faith, etc.

Again, faith cannot be lost, but assurance may; therefore assurance is not faith. Though assurance is a precious#footnote[Ps 51:12. Ps 30:6, 7.] flower in the garden of a saint, and is infinitely more sweet and delightful to the soul than all outward comforts#footnote[Song 5:6. Isa 8:17.] and contentments, yet it is only a flower that is subject to fade, and to lose its freshness#footnote[There are many thousands of precious souls, of whom this world is not worthy, who have the faith of reliance and yet lack assurance and its effects, such as high joy, glorious peace, and vehement longings for the coming of Christ.] and beauty, as saints find by sad experience, etc.

Again, a man must first have faith before he can have assurance; therefore assurance is not faith. And that a man must first have faith before he can have assurance is clear from this: a man must first be saved before he can be assured of his salvation, for he cannot be assured of what does not exist; and a man must first have a saving faith before he can be saved by faith, for he cannot be saved by what he does not have. Therefore a man must first have faith before he can have assurance, and so it plainly follows that assurance is not faith, etc.
